---
// About page: what AFG is, why it does both policy and teaching, how it
// started, what it does and how, its impact, the people who lead it, and
// what's next. The People section replaces the old Team page.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageTop from '../components/PageTop.astro';
import SummaryList from '../components/SummaryList.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import '../styles/pages/about.css';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import { facts } from '../data/facts';

const f = facts;

// The people who lead AFG (src/content/team/). Anyone without a bio is
// listed without the toggle.
const people = await Promise.all(
  (await getCollection('team'))
    .filter((m) => !m.data.draft)
    .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)
    .map(async (m) => ({
      name: m.data.name,
      role: m.data.role,
      Bio: m.body?.trim() ? (await render(m)).Content : null,
    }))
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  pageClass="page-about"
  description="AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in June 2023. We write policy recommendations for the government and run free tutoring for children from underprivileged families."
>
  <PageTop vertical="關於我們">
    <h1>Policy and teaching, for the same reason.</h1>
    <!-- 1. What AFG is -->
    <p class="lead">
      AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in {f.founded.en}. We do two things.
      We write recommendations on AI and economic policy and send them to the Hong Kong government,
      about {f.recsPerYear.word.en} a year. We also run free tutoring for primary school children from
      underprivileged families, and have taught {f.children.value} of them so far.
    </p>
  </PageTop>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="at-a-glance">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="at-a-glance">At a glance</h2>
      </div>
      <SummaryList
        rows={[
          { key: 'Founded', value: `${f.founded.en}, by ${f.founders[0]}, ${f.founders[1]} and ${f.founders[2]}` },
          { key: 'Members', value: `${f.members.value} students` },
          {
            key: 'Policy',
            value: `About ${f.recsPerYear.value.replace('~', '')} recommendations a year; every Policy Address and Budget consultation since ${f.recsPerYear.since}`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Teaching',
            value: `${f.children.value} children, ${f.schools.value} schools, ${f.tutors.value} trained tutors, ${f.modules.length} modules`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Outcome',
            value: `<a href="/policy#ai-for-all">${f.aiForAll.amount.en} for AI for All</a> in the ${f.aiForAll.budget} Budget`,
          },
          {
            key: 'Partners',
            value: "Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, Kennedy Town School, St. Barnabas' Home &amp; Society and 10% Organisation",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Why both: the connective idea -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-both">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <p class="kicker-date">Why we do both</p>
        <h2 id="why-both">People who write policy should meet the people it affects.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <blockquote class="tagline">
          AI policy written by people who have never met the people it affects isn't good policy. The
          children we teach are the people it affects.
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          It isn't a coincidence that we do both. Sit in a classroom in Ho Man Tin and watch a child use an
          AI tutor for the first time, and you learn things a consultation paper won't tell you: which
          children already have a tutor at home, which ones have a device, and which ones are about to fall
          further behind because of a technology everyone keeps calling an equaliser.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of our submissions start there. Teaching shows us where the gaps are. Research tells us
          whether a gap is a policy problem. Then the submission asks the government to fix it. It works
          the other way too: what we learn writing about education and labour policy changes what we think
          is worth teaching a ten-year-old. We'd rather be the people who have been in the room than the
          people who have only read about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Origin -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-it-started">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="how-it-started">How it started</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="prose">
          AI For Good was founded in {f.founded.en} by <strong>Justin Lo</strong>,{' '}
          <strong>Ka Hei Ng</strong> and <strong>Oscar Zheng</strong>. <strong>David Dong</strong> and{' '}
          <strong>Jackie Xue</strong> joined later. We started outside school, as an independent think
          tank rather than a school club. Since then we've grown to more than 35 members and started
          working with schools across Hong Kong, which is how the tutoring programme reaches the children
          it does.
        </p>
        <figure class="origin-figure">
          <Photo
            name="team-outside-school.jpg"
            alt="Five AI For Good members outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School."
            sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 46rem, 100vw"
          />
          <figcaption>Outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, where we teach.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Justin's own account, in the first person and left in his words.
       The voice shift from "we" to "I" is deliberate and signed. -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-i-started">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="why-i-started">Why I started it</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Why the policy work</h3>
        <p>
          AI arrived in Hong Kong faster than the city could decide who it was for. I watched it show up in
          my school, then in my friends' internships, then in the government's plans, and the pattern was
          always the same: the people who already had the most access got it first. Nobody was choosing
          that. It was just what happened when nobody was asked to choose. I had spent two years learning
          how public money gets allocated and who ends up bearing the cost of a decision, and it seemed
          absurd to sit on that while the decisions were being made a few kilometres away. So we started
          writing to the people making them.
        </p>
        <h3>Why the teaching</h3>
        <p>
          This started as a group of us at school teaching each other AI, mostly by playing with it. We
          built things that didn't work and a few that did, and it was obvious how much you could get out
          of these tools once someone showed you the first step. Then it became obvious who wasn't getting
          shown. The children with the most to gain from AI were the least likely to ever be handed it,
          and that gap wasn't going to close on its own. So we took what we'd been doing for fun in a
          classroom and brought it to schools where nobody else was offering it.
        </p>
        <p class="signed">Justin Lo, Founder &amp; Director</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. What we do: the two halves, each in its own colour -->
  <section aria-labelledby="what-we-do">
    <div class="wrap what-head">
      <h2 id="what-we-do">What we do</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="halves">
      <div class="half-col tone-policy">
        <div class="half washed block">
          <h3>Policy</h3>
          <p>
            Every year we send about {f.recsPerYear.word.en} written recommendations to the Hong Kong
            government. We've responded to every Policy Address and Budget consultation since 2024, and
            we're now working on a submission to the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social
            Development (2026–2030). Our topics come out of the work itself: tax, AI governance,
            education, jobs and health. Over several submissions we argued for an inclusive AI programme,
            and the {f.aiForAll.budget} Budget set aside {f.aiForAll.amount.en} for AI for All.
          </p>
          <a class="textlink" href="/policy">Read our policy work</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="half-col tone-teaching">
        <div class="half washed block">
          <h3>Teaching</h3>
          <p>
            We run free tutoring for primary school children from underprivileged families in seven
            schools across Hong Kong. Our {f.tutors.value} trained tutors teach four modules: maths,
            English, economics and science. Each one uses AI as a free, patient study partner. We've
            taught {f.children.value} children so far, and since {f.bandUp.since},{' '}
            {f.bandUp.value}{f.bandUp.unit} of our students have moved up at least one class band.
          </p>
          <a class="textlink" href="/teaching">See our teaching</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. How we work -->
  <section class="sec how-we-work" aria-labelledby="how-we-work">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="how-we-work">How we work</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="methods">
        <div>
          <dt>Writing a proposal</dt>
          <dd>
            First we talk to our members, then we research, then we write. Every proposal sets out the
            case for, then the case against, then our recommendation, always in that order. If the case
            against turns out to be stronger, we don't send it.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Running a lesson</dt>
          <dd>
            Every tutor is trained before they teach. They follow a written curriculum built around the
            four modules, so a child gets the same quality of lesson whichever school they're at and
            whoever is teaching.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Impact: the account first, then the two outcomes it rests on -->
  <section class="sec impact" aria-labelledby="our-impact">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="our-impact">Our impact</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AI For Good started in 2023 as a small group of students. Today there are more than 35 of us,
          and we all believe young people can change things by speaking up about them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each year we send around 20 policy recommendations to the Hong Kong government. We've taken
          part in the Policy Address and Budget consultations since 2024, and we're now contributing to
          the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development (2026–2030). Alongside the policy
          work, we've taught {f.children.value} children from underprivileged families at our partner
          schools and trained {f.tutors.value} student tutors. Since {f.bandUp.since},{' '}
          {f.bandUp.value}{f.bandUp.unit} of the students we teach have moved up at least one class band.
        </p>
        <p>
          For us, the policy and the teaching come from the same belief: a better world gets built by
          people who decide not to wait.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="halves">
    <div class="half-col tone-policy">
      <div class="half band">
        <p class="proof-figure">HK$50M</p>
        <p class="proof-label">
          allocated to AI for All in the {f.aiForAll.budget} Budget, the kind of inclusive AI programme
          we'd been arguing for
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="half-col tone-teaching">
      <div class="half band">
        <p class="proof-figure">{f.bandUp.value}{f.bandUp.unit}</p>
        <p class="proof-label">of our students have moved up at least one class band since {f.bandUp.since}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The people who lead it. This was the Team page. -->
  <section class="sec sec-after-band" aria-labelledby="people">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="people">Run entirely by students.</h2>
        <p>
          More than 35 of us work on policy research and tutoring. These are the people who lead the
          work. Press a name to read more about them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <!-- Each name opens its bio: a native <details>, so it works from the
             keyboard and without JavaScript. -->
        <div class="people">
          {
            people.map(({ name, role, Bio }) =>
              Bio ? (
                <details class="person">
                  <summary>
                    <span class="person-head">
                      <h3>{name}</h3>
                      <span class="person-role">{role}</span>
                    </span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="prose">
                    <Bio />
                  </div>
                </details>
              ) : (
                <div class="person">
                  <span class="person-head">
                    <h3>{name}</h3>
                    <span class="person-role">{role}</span>
                  </span>
                </div>
              )
            )
          }
        </div>
        <figure class="origin-figure">
          <Photo
            name="team-classroom.jpg"
            alt="Six AI For Good members in a classroom at Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, after a session."
            sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 46rem, 100vw"
          />
          <figcaption>In a classroom at Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, after a session.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Partners -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="partners">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="partners">Who we work with</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        We teach with Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, St. Barnabas' Home &amp; Society, Kennedy Town
        School and 10% Organisation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Where we're going -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="whats-next">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="whats-next">What's next</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="next-list">
        <li class="tone-policy">
          <span class="tone-tag">Policy</span>
          <span>
            We're working on our submission to the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social
            Development (2026–2030), about how AI and economic policy can open up opportunities rather
            than narrow them.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="tone-teaching">
          <span class="tone-tag">Teaching</span>
          <span>
            We're going back to Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School next year with all four modules and a
            new group of tutors.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Get involved -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="get-involved">
    <div class="wrap rail">
      <div class="rail-head">
        <h2 id="get-involved">Get involved</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Schools and organisations:</strong> if you work with children from underprivileged
          families and could use tutors, we'll bring the lessons, the tutors and the equipment.{' '}
          <a href="/get-involved#schools">Get in touch</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Students:</strong> we're recruiting at Victoria Shanghai Academy. If you're into
          policy, economics or AI, we care more about what you want to do than about titles.{' '}
          <a href="/get-involved#students">Tell us what you'd like to work on</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
